Detecting overfitting from training and validation curves, the iterative regularize-and-tune loop, information leakage into the validation process, training the final model on all data and evaluating once on the test set, and the opening of 6.3 Deploying your model. Once a model has statistical power and can overfit, spend most of your time maximizing generalization: iterate over architecture, dropout, L1/L2 regularization, hyperparameters and data, judged on validation data only. Each round of tuning leaks a little information about the validation set into the model, so after settling on a configuration, retrain on training plus validation data and evaluate once on the test set. A large gap between test and validation scores means the validation procedure was unreliable or was overfitted. The page finishes **6.2.4**: always monitor training and validation loss, plus training and validation values of every metric you care about. When validation performance starts to degrade, you have achieved overfitting.

**6.2.5 Regularizing and tuning your model.** With statistical power and the ability to overfit, you are on the right path, and the goal becomes **maximizing generalization performance**. This phase takes the most time: modify the model, train, evaluate on *validation* data (not test data), and repeat until the model is as good as it can get. Things to try:

- different architectures; add or remove layers;
- dropout;
- L1 or L2 regularization if the model is small;
- different hyperparameters (units per layer, optimizer learning rate);
- optionally, more data curation or feature engineering: collect and annotate more data, build better features, remove uninformative ones.

Much of this can be automated with **automated hyperparameter-tuning software** such as **KerasTuner** (chapter 18).

A caution: every time validation feedback guides a change, you **leak information about the validation process into the model**. A few times is harmless; done systematically over many iterations, the model ends up *overfitting to the validation process* even though it never trains on validation data, and evaluation becomes less reliable.

Once the configuration is satisfactory, **train the final production model on all available data (training + validation)** and evaluate it **one last time on the test set**. If test performance is significantly worse than validation performance, either the validation procedure was not reliable or you overfitted the validation data while tuning; consider a more reliable protocol such as **iterated K-fold validation**.

**6.3 Deploying your model** begins: after clearing the final test evaluation, the model is ready to be deployed and begin its productive life. Overfitting is reached when validation loss or metrics begin to degrade; monitor both training and validation curves for every metric you care about. Regularizing and tuning takes the most time: iterate on architecture, dropout, L1/L2 (small models), hyperparameters and data. Tune on validation data, never on test data; KerasTuner can automate much of the search (chapter 18). Systematic tuning leaks validation information into the model and makes validation scores optimistic. Finish by training on training + validation data and evaluating once on the test set. A large test/validation gap means an unreliable or overfitted validation procedure; consider iterated K-fold.

Finishing the REST-API deployment option (self-hosted vs. managed cloud) and introducing on-device deployment: when to use it and which tools (export(), TensorFlow Lite, ONNX runtime) make it possible. Where a model runs is an engineering decision driven by connectivity, latency, data sensitivity, and device resources. A REST API suits most of the chapter's example projects, but when latency, offline use or privacy dominate, the model must run on the device itself, usually as a smaller, slightly less accurate model exported via `export()` to TensorFlow Lite or ONNX. This page closes the discussion of **deploying a model as a REST API** and opens the next option, **deploying a model on a device**.

It first places the chapter's running projects: the image search engine, the music recommender, credit card fraud detection and the satellite imagery project all fit REST serving well. None of them needs offline use, sub-second answers or strict data confidentiality, which were the conditions listed on p.209. The authors then raise a practical question: do you **host the serving code yourself**, or use a **fully managed third-party cloud service**? Their example is Google's Cloud AI Platform. You upload a TensorFlow model to Google Cloud Storage (GCS) and get back an API endpoint, and the service handles *batching predictions, load balancing, and scaling*.

The second half covers on-device deployment. The model runs on the same hardware as the application: a smartphone, an embedded ARM CPU on a robot, or a microcontroller. A camera that detects faces is the familiar example. The book gives four conditions for choosing this setup:

1. strict **latency** constraints or a **low-connectivity** environment (e.g., immersive AR);
2. the model can be made **small enough** for the device's memory and power budget;
3. top accuracy is **not mission critical**, since efficiency and accuracy trade off against each other;
4. the input data is **strictly sensitive** and must not be decryptable on a server.

Two running projects need this option. The spam detector must run on the user's phone because the chat app is end-to-end encrypted. The bad-cookie detector has strict latency constraints and must run at the factory, though there are no power or space limits there, so it can use a GPU.

On tooling, the same `export()` method produces a TensorFlow or ONNX save that includes the computation graph. **TensorFlow Lite** runs such models on Android, iOS, ARM CPUs, Raspberry Pi and some microcontrollers, using the same saved-model format as TensorFlow Serving. The **ONNX runtime** also runs on mobile devices. Image search, music recommendation, fraud detection and satellite imagery are all good fits for REST serving. REST serving can be self-hosted or managed; managed services like Cloud AI Platform handle batching, load balancing and scaling. Deploy on device when latency or connectivity is critical, the model can be made small, peak accuracy is not mission critical, or data must not be decrypted remotely. Efficiency and accuracy trade off: device constraints often mean shipping a less accurate model. The spam filter goes on-device for privacy; the cookie detector goes on-site for latency, and it can still use a GPU. export() produces TensorFlow or ONNX saves; TensorFlow Lite and the ONNX runtime run them on phones and embedded hardware.

Running models directly in the browser or in desktop JavaScript apps (TensorFlow.js, ONNX's JavaScript runtime), and the start of inference-time optimization (weight pruning). Running a model in the user's browser saves server cost, keeps data local, avoids the network round trip and works offline. It requires a model small enough for consumer hardware and one that need not stay confidential, and the model should be optimized (e.g., pruned) before it is shipped. The page covers the third deployment target, **deploying a model in the browser**. Deep learning is common in browser-based and desktop JavaScript applications. Such apps *can* call a remote model over REST, but running the model directly in the browser on the user's computer (using the GPU if one is available) has key advantages. The authors recommend it when:

- you want to **offload compute** to the end user, which can dramatically cut server costs;
- the **input data must stay** on the user's computer or phone, as with the web and desktop versions of the spam-detection chat app, a cross-platform JavaScript app;
- you have **strict latency** requirements: the local model may be slower than a big server GPU, but there is no extra ~100 ms network round trip;
- the app must **keep working offline** once the model has been downloaded and cached.

There are two caveats. The model must be small enough not to *hog* the user's CPU, GPU or RAM. And because the entire model is downloaded, **nothing about it can be confidential**. The authors also warn that a trained model usually lets someone recover *some information about its training data*, so a model trained on sensitive data should not be made public.

On tooling, the TensorFlow ecosystem has **TensorFlow.js**, and ONNX has a native JavaScript runtime. TensorFlow.js implements almost all of the Keras API (its working name was *WebKeras*) plus many lower-level TensorFlow APIs, and a saved Keras model can be imported into it for use in a browser app or an Electron desktop app.

The next subsection, **Inference model optimization**, argues that optimization matters most under tight power and memory budgets (phones, embedded devices) or low-latency requirements, and should always happen *before* importing into TensorFlow.js or exporting to TensorFlow Lite. The first of two techniques, **weight pruning**, begins at the bottom of the page. Not every coefficient in a weight tensor contributes equally, so keeping only the most significant ones considerably reduces the parameter count. The text continues on p.212, which also introduces weight quantization. Run a model in the browser to offload compute, keep data local, avoid ~100 ms network latency, or work offline after caching. Only do this if the model won't hog the user's CPU/GPU/RAM and nothing about it needs to stay confidential. Trained models can leak information about their training data, so don't publish models trained on sensitive data. TensorFlow.js (formerly WebKeras) implements almost all of Keras; ONNX also has a native JavaScript runtime. Optimize models for inference before converting to TensorFlow.js or TensorFlow Lite. Weight pruning keeps only the most significant coefficients to shrink the model (continued on p.212).

The end of the incremental-summary debugging workflow for Sequential models, the limitations of Sequential, and a first Functional-API model (Listing 7.8). Sequential models only express single-input, single-output linear stacks; real models often need multiple inputs, multiple outputs or non-linear topologies, so most Keras models use the Functional API, in which you declare an Input, call layers on it, and wrap inputs and outputs in keras.Model. The page opens with the tail of the summary from Listing 7.7's model (p.218), then shows the next step of the workflow: after `model.add(layers.Dense(10, activation="softmax"))`, `summary()` of `"sequential_2"` now lists `dense_4` `(None, 64)` 256 params and `dense_5` `(None, 10)` 650 params, **906 total (3.54 KB)**. The authors note that watching the output shape evolve this way is **a common debugging workflow for layers that transform their inputs in complex ways**, such as the convolutional layers of chapter 8.

**Section 7.2.2, The Functional API**, begins by stating the Sequential model's limitation bluntly: its applicability is *extremely limited* — it can only express models with **a single input and a single output**, applying one layer after the other. Yet in practice models often have **multiple inputs** (e.g. an image and its metadata), **multiple outputs** (different things to predict about the data), or a **nonlinear topology**. For those you use the Functional API — what *most Keras models you'll encounter in the wild* use; the authors compare it to playing with LEGO bricks.

**A simple example.** Listing 7.8 rewrites the familiar two-layer stack functionally:

1. `inputs = keras.Input(shape=(3,), name="my_input")` — declare the input (with a name);
2. `features = layers.Dense(64, activation="relu")(inputs)` — create a layer and *call it* on the input;
3. `outputs = layers.Dense(10, activation="softmax")(features)` — chain another layer;
4. `model = keras.Model(inputs=inputs, outputs=outputs, name="my_functional_model")` — build the model from its inputs and outputs.

The page then starts the step-by-step explanation with the first line, noting that Input objects can be named like everything else. The explanation of what `inputs` is (a symbolic tensor) follows on p.220. Incremental summary() checks are a common debugging tool, especially for convolutional layers (chapter 8). Sequential models are limited to one input, one output, and a linear chain of layers. Multiple inputs, multiple outputs, or nonlinear topology call for the Functional API. Most Keras models in the wild use the Functional API. Functional pattern: Input → call layers on tensors → keras.Model(inputs, outputs).

