The last lines of Listing 10.25 (blending the colorized Grad-CAM heatmap onto the elephant photo and saving it), what the class-activation heatmap tells you, and the opening of section 10.4: the idea of visualizing a model's latent space by recording a layer's activations and projecting them to 2D. Grad-CAM answers why the network predicted a class and where that class is in the image. A different inspection technique looks at the model as a whole: every deep model embeds inputs in a latent manifold where nearby points are semantically similar, and you can view that manifold by recording a late layer's activations and projecting those high-dimensional points onto a 2D surface while keeping as much of their organization as possible. The page opens with the end of **Listing 10.25** (started on p.329). The jet-colorized heatmap `jet_heatmap` is added to the original image with weight 0.4 (`jet_heatmap * 0.4 + img`), so the heatmap shows at roughly 40% opacity over the photo. The float array is turned back into a PIL image with `keras.utils.array_to_img` and saved as `elephant_cam.jpg`.

The authors then say what the technique gives you. A class activation heatmap answers two questions: **why** the network thought the image contained an African elephant, and **where** the elephant is in the picture. They point out that the elephant calf's **ears** are strongly activated and suggest ("probably") that this is how the network tells African elephants from Indian elephants. African elephants do have much larger ears.

**Section 10.4** introduces the last inspection technique of the chapter, **latent space visualization**. All deep learning models embed their inputs in a *latent space*, a manifold where points that are close represent semantically similar inputs and where classes become separable, which is what makes classification possible. To see this manifold you:

1. pick a layer, typically the one just before the classification layer;
2. record its activations for a set of inputs;
3. treat each input's activation vector as the coordinates of a point on the manifold;
4. plot the points.

Any layer works, since each layer encodes its own latent space, and these spaces become more semantically organized deeper in the model. Using the paper-ball analogy from chapters 2 and 5, each layer's latent space is a snapshot of the ball partway through being uncrumpled, so the penultimate layer, where the sheet is almost flat, is the most informative.

The problem is that people can only interpret 2D, 3D, maybe 4D pictures, while these activation vectors typically have hundreds or thousands of dimensions. The points must be **projected onto a 2D surface** in a way that keeps as much of the manifold's original organization as possible. The next page (p.331) argues that this is possible because the manifold's intrinsic dimensionality is much lower than its ambient dimension. Listing 10.25 ends by adding the jet-colored heatmap to the photo at weight 0.4 and saving elephant_cam.jpg. A class activation heatmap answers why the network chose a class and where the object is. The strongly activated calf ears probably explain how the network separates African from Indian elephants. Every deep model embeds inputs in a latent space where proximity means semantic similarity and classes are separable. Visualize it by recording a layer's activations (typically the penultimate layer) for many inputs and plotting them as points. Each layer has its own latent space; deeper layers are more semantically organized, like a paper ball nearer to flat. Activation vectors have hundreds or thousands of dimensions, so they must be projected to 2D while keeping as much structure as possible.

